## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical virtual memory implementation skills including COW semantics, page table management, reference counting, synchronization, and TLB consistency. It evaluates the ability to handle concurrent page faults without memory leaks or race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Copy-On-Write (COW) optimization
- Page table updates and TLB invalidation
- Race condition handling and synchronization primitives
- Concurrent memory management

## Code Example
```c
void handle_write_fault(PTE *pte) {
  acquire(pte->lock);
  if (pte->refcount > 1) {
    Page *newpage = alloc_page();
    memcpy(newpage->addr, pte->frame->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    pte->frame = newpage;
    pte->writable = 1;
    pte->refcount--;
    invalidate_tlb(pte->vaddr);
  } else {
    pte->writable = 1;
  }
  release(pte->lock);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests understanding of probabilistic scheduling, fairness, and simple kernel-like data paths without overwhelming complexity.

## Key Concepts
- Lottery scheduling basics; per-process tickets; cumulative distribution; random number generation; blocking/wake interactions.
- Data structures: ready list with ticket counts; total_tickets; small RNG; race-free updates.
- Testing: compare empirical waiting time and starvation under IO-bound vs CPU-bound mixes.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  int pid;
  int tickets;
  int state; // READY/BLOCKED
} proc_t;

static uint32_t rng = 12345;
static uint32_t lcg(void){ rng = 1664525u*rng + 1013904223u; return rng; }

proc_t* select_next(proc_t** procs, int n){
  int total = 0; for(int i=0;i<n;i++) if(procs[i]->state==READY) total += procs[i]->tickets;
  if(total<=0) return NULL;
  int r = lcg() % total + 1;
  int acc = 0; for(int i=0;i<n;i++) if(procs[i]->state==READY){ acc += procs[i]->tickets; if(acc >= r) return procs[i]; }
  return NULL;
}
```

